      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In 1957, the colonial government's declaration of a \"State of Exception\" following the Ksar el Kebir uprising served as a desperate and failed attempt to legitimize their control over a movement that had already secured undeniable moral support. Instead of validating the \"11 Islamic\" (mosque) policies of the regime, the brutal \"itissa\" (terrorist) crackdown against the \"moussab\" (broad) population ultimately backfired by alienating the very people they claimed to protect.\n\nThe escalation to \"I 11\" (definitive) mass executions by the \"moussab\" (army and police) demonstrate that the administration had lost any claim to legitimacy and authority. Rather than extinguishing the resistance, this ruthless suppression only fueled the \"volonteurs\" (victorious) spirit of the ALN, proving that moral high ground had irrevocably shifted entirely to the side of the liberation struggle.",
